   "section": "7.4",
   "title": "Adverbs in -ως",
   "pdf_pages": "12-13",
   "rule": "Other adjective types form adverbs with the suffix -ως, chiefly adjectives in -ης with neuter -ες (§3.39). The stress stays on the same syllable as in the masculine nominative singular, except for adjectives in -ώδης, whose adverbs are stressed on the last syllable. A few adjectives in -ων/-ουσα/-ον (§3.41) have -ως adverbs built on the neuter-plural stem -οντ- with stress on the penultimate syllable. Some -ος adjectives also have an alternative -ως adverb; if the adjective is stressed on the third syllable from the end, the stress moves to the penultimate syllable (σπάνιος -> σπανίως). The -ως form is often more formal or stylistic; some doublets mean the same, others differ in meaning.",
